xtrapolation; matches the strong-target degradation seen in the run.\"\n537\t        },\n538\t        {\n539\t          \"category\": \"baseline_comparator\",\n540\t          \"query\": \"predicting success of new LLM on unseen instances from few reference instances assessor\",\n541\t          \"rationale\": \"Instance-level predictor trained from reference-instance outcomes on HELM-Lite; related prediction-from-subset formulation.\"\n542\t        },\n543\t        {\n544\t          \"category\": \"core_method\",\n545\t          \"query\": \"model-assisted survey estimation difference estimator generalized regression estimator\",\n546\t          \"rationale\": \"The core estimator is a model-assisted difference estimator (model prediction plus subset residual correction); needs survey-sampling grounding.\"\n547\t        },\n548\t        {\n549\t          \"category\": \"core_method\",\n550\t          \"query\": \"D-optimal experimental design Fisher information logistic regression optimal item selection\",\n551\t          \"rationale\": \"Item selection is D-optimal on latent features using logistic Fisher information; needs optimal-design and IRT test-design grounding.\"\n552\t        },\n553\t        {\n554\t          \"category\": \"architectural_shift\",\n555\t          \"query\": \"stacked generalization non-negative least squares combining predictors\",\n556\t          \"rationale\": \"Final estimator stacks candidate estimators with NNLS weights over source pseudo-target folds.\"\n557\t        },\n558\t        {\n559\t          \"category\": \"benchmark_context\",\n560\t          \"query\": \"Open LLM Leaderboard v2 IFEval BBH MMLU-Pro GPQA MuSR benchmarks\",\n561\t          \"rationale\": \"Data come from per-item Open LLM Leaderboard v2 results and HELM; benchmark citations needed for dataset description.\"\n562\t        }\n563\t      ],\n564\t      \"result_driven_themes\": [\n565\t        \"Estimation error and rank correlation degrade much more for strong (out-of-range) target models than for randomly held-out targets (strong_targets MAE roughly 2x random_targets; Spearman ~0.77-0.82 vs ~0.96)\",\n566\t        \"Gains over random-subset and anchor-point baselines are small in absolute MAE terms (about 0.007-0.011) but consistent across the three variants\",\n567\t        \"Source-only validation / stacking for extrapolation to targets above the source ability range\",\n568\t        \"Posterior-mean and tail-calibration did not improve over plain logistic Fisher D-opt stacking (0.9445 vs 0.9452 combined score)\"\n569\t      ],\n570\t      \"status\": \"ready\"\n571\t    },\n572\t    {\n573\t      \"architectural_shifts\": [\n574\t        \"From clustering/anchor-based item selection to latent-space D-optimal selection\",\n575\t        \"From a single-model estimator to a stacked ensemble over source pseudo-target folds with incumbent fallback\",\n576\t        \"From a linear/embedding latent model to a logistic latent-abil\n577\t\n578\t[truncated after 14000 chars]\n579\t```\n580\t\n581\t## manuscript source excerpt\n582\t\n583\t```tex\n584\t\\documentclass{article}\n585\t\n586\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n587\t\\usepackage{xfp}\n588\t\\usepackage{draftwatermark}\n589\t\\newlength{\\watermarkTextWidth}\n590\t\\AddToHook{begindocument/end}{\n591\t  \\SetWatermarkText{\\normalfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}\n592\t  \\SetWatermarkFontSize{10pt}\n593\t  \\SetWatermarkLightness{0.85}\n594\t  \\settowidth{\\watermarkTextWidth}{\\normalfont\\fontsize{10pt}{12pt}\\selectfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}\n595\t  \\edef\\watermarkScale{\\fpeval{0.8 * sqrt(\\paperwidth**2 + \\paperheight**2) / \\watermarkTextWidth}}\n596\t  \\edef\\watermarkAngle{\\fpeval{atand(\\paperheight/\\paperwidth)}}\n597\t  \\SetWatermarkScale{\\watermarkScale}\n598\t  \\SetWatermarkAngle{\\watermarkAngle}\n599\t}\n600\t\n601\t \n602\t\\usepackage{paper,times}\n603\t\n604\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n605\t\\usepackage{url}\n606\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n607\t\\usepackage{subcaption}\n608\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n609\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n610\t\n611\t\\title{Source-Validated Latent-Ability Stacking for Few-Item Benchmark Score Prediction: Small, Statistically Unresolved Gains and a Persistent Extrapolation Gap}\n612\t\n613\t\\author{Anonymous authors}\n614\t\n615\t\\newcommand{\\ours}{\\textsc{Stack}}\n616\t\n617\t\\begin{document}\n618\t\\maketitle\n619\t\n620\t\\begin{abstract}\n621\tEvaluating a language model on every item of a benchmark is expensive, so a natural shortcut is to run the model on a small item subset and extrapolate its full-benchmark accuracy, using the per-item results of previously evaluated ``source'' models to choose the subset. We study this setting with binary per-item results, a budget of $k{=}100$ items, and two kinds of new model: targets that resemble the sources and targets stronger than every source. We evaluate a configuration that fits a logistic latent-ability model to the source matrix, picks items by greedy Fisher D-optimal design at upper-tail abilities, estimates target ability from the $k$ answers, and combines a calibrated subset mean with latent-model estimates through non-negative stacking weights fitted only on source pseudo-targets. On five held-out benchmarks (three seeds), it attains a mean absolute error (MAE) of 0.0294 against 0.0339 for a random-subset mean, 0.0428 for $k$-means anchor points and 0.0692 for an IRT-style estimator; its predictions also degrade as expected under both negative controls (single episodes; no pass/fail criterion is evidenced). However, the paired bootstrap interval for the gain over the best baseline includes zero (MAE gain $+0.0045$, 95\\% CI $[-0.0052, +0.0134]$), so we do not claim an improvement. Error roughly doubles and rank correlation falls from 0.956 to 0.730 for targets stronger than all sources. The study is limited to one budget, binary scores, and no single-factor ablations; the extrapolation gap is the largest measured difference, reported as point estimates without an interval.\n622\t\\end{abstract}\n623\t\n624\t\\section{Introduction}\n625\tReporting a language model's score on a benchmark normally requires answering every item. When many checkpoints, benchmarks, prompt formats or inference settings must be compared, this cost multiplies, and several lines of work therefore evaluate on a small subset of items and predict the full-benchmark score \\citep{Perlitz2023EfficientB,Vivek2023AnchorPB,Polo2024tinyBenchmarksEL,Kipnis2024metabenchA}. In many practical settings the evaluator also holds a historical matrix of per-item correct/incorrect outcomes for earlier ``source'' models. This matrix can inform which items to keep and how to turn a new model's subset results into a full-benchmark estimate.\n626\t\n627\tThe hardest and most practically relevant case is a new model that is \\emph{better than all sources}: its ability lies outside the range spanned by the historical matrix, so any estimator that is tuned to source models must extrapolate. A method that works for models resembling the sources may degrade exactly where evaluation savings matter most, namely when comparing new, stronger systems. Predicting the behaviour of more capable models from results on weaker ones is a recognized and difficult target elsewhere in language-model evaluation---observational scaling laws, for example, fit capability measures on existing models and extrapolate to more capable ones \\citep{Ruan2024ObservationalSL}---but how well few-item benchmark prediction holds up out of range is not settled by those results, and it is the question we probe here. We therefore study the problem under two explicit target regimes and report them separately.\n628\t\n629\t\\paragraph{Question.} With only the source models' binary per-item results and the $k{=}100$ answers of a new model, does choosing items by Fisher D-optimal design on a source-fitted logistic latent model, and predicting with a stacked estimator validated only on source pseudo-targets, predict full-benchmark accuracy and model ranking better than (i) a random-subset mean, (ii) $k$-means anchor points, and (iii) an IRT-style estimator---and does it help for targets stronger than every source?\n630\t\n631\t\\paragraph{Answer.} Measured on five benchmarks disjoint from those used to develop the configuration, the point estimates favour the stacked configuration on MAE against all three baselines and on utility against the best baseline (baseline RMSE, Spearman and Kendall were not retained), but the evidence does not resolve the gain over the best baseline (a plain random-subset mean): the paired bootstrap interval includes zero. The largest measured difference, reported as point estimates without an interval, is a gap between regimes: MAE is 0.0409 for stronger-than-source targets versus 0.0179 for random targets, and Spearman correlation is 0.730 versus 0.956. The same pattern appears on the five development benchmarks. Part of this gap may reflect the narrower score range of strong targets rather than extrapolation as such, which we did not test.\n632\t\n633\t\\paragraph{Contributions.} (1) A precisely specified estimator family that combines a logistic latent-ability model, Fisher D-optimal item design at upper-tail reference abilities, and non-negative stacking validated on source-only pseudo-targets with strongest-holdout folds for the extrapolation regime (Section~\\ref{sec:method}). (2) A controlled held-out comparison against three matched baselines with a paired hierarchical bootstrap and two negative controls (Section~\\ref{sec:results}). (3) A measured characterization of the extrapolation gap on development and held-out benchmark groups, together with an explicit list of what the design cannot attribute (no single-factor ablation) and what was not tested (Section~\\ref{sec:discussion}). We stress that this is a calibrated empirical study, not a demonstration of a decisive method improvement.\n634\t\n635\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n636\t\\centering\n637\t\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/overview_protocol.png}\n638\t\\caption{Overview of the few-item prediction protocol and the evaluated configuration. A binary source matrix is used to fit a latent model, choose $k{=}100$ items, and fit stacking weights on source pseudo-targets; a new model answers only the selected items, and a stacked estimator predicts its full-benchmark accuracy. Targets are either random held-out models or models stronger than every source.}\n639\t\\label{fig:overview}\n640\t\\end{figure}\n641\t\n642\t\\section{Related Work}\n643\t\\paragraph{Subset selection for benchmark prediction.} Anchor points \\citep{Vivek2023AnchorPB} cluster items by the response vectors of source models and score a new model by a cluster-size-weighted average over representative items. tinyBenchmarks \\citep{Polo2024tinyBenchmarksEL} combines such anchors with item response theory (IRT) to predict full scores, and metabench \\citep{Kipnis2024metabenchA} uses IRT-based information to select items and fits a predictor on top of them. Efficient benchmarking \\citep{Perlitz2023EfficientB} studies how much a large benchmark can be reduced while preserving model rankings, and PromptEval \\citep{Polo2024EfficientME} applies related methods to the variation introduced by prompt formats. ONEBench \\citep{Ghosh2024ONEBenchTT} studies how to aggregate sparse sample-level measurements into model scores and rankings. Our baselines follow the clustering and IRT families; the baseline implementations are the evaluator's own re-implementations rather than the original code.\n644\t\n645\t\\paragraph{Learned and adaptive prediction.} Pacchiardi et al.\\ \\citep{Pacchiardi2024100II} train an assessor that predicts a new model's success on unseen instances from its results on a few reference instances. Active evaluation acquisition \\citep{Li2024ActiveEA} learns a policy that chooses items adaptively and predicts the rest. Adaptive testing in the psychometric tradition \\citep{Zhuang2023PositionAE} and its extension to continuous scores \\citep{Balkir2026ConfidentRW} choose items sequentially with stopping rules. Our setting is static: the $k$ items are chosen before seeing the target, from source data alone. A comparison of several benchmark-prediction methods across many benchmarks is reported by Zhang et al.\\ \\citep{Zhang2025HowBP}, whose released per-item score matrices we use as data (Section~\\ref{sec:protocol}); we make no claim about their methods or conclusions beyond what the matrices contain.\n646\t\n647\t\\paragraph{Estimation and design tools.} The latent model is an IRT-style logistic model \\citep{Rodriguez2021EvaluationEA} fitted to a model-by-item matrix; with a vector of abilities per model and item-specific loadings it is a compensatory multidimensional IRT model, whose item information and ability estimation are treated in \\citet{Reckase2009MultidimensionalIR}, and the item-information criterion we use for selection is the same quantity that drives item selection in adaptive testing \\citep{Zhuang2023PositionAE}. The final estimator follows the model-assisted approach of survey sampling, which corrects a model-based prediction with the mean residual of an observed sample, and which has been extended to flexible machine-learned predictors \\citep{Breidt2017ModelAssistedSE}; prediction-powered inference \\citep{Angelopoulos2023PredictionpoweredI} is a recent descendant of the same principle. Item selection by greedy log-determinant maximization is D-optimal experimental design \\citep{Kiefer1960TheEO}, and the combination of estimators with non-negative weights fitted on held-out predictions is stacked generalization \\citep{Wolpert1992StackedG}. Benchmark scores themselves vary with seeds and prompts \\citep{Madaan2024QuantifyingVI}, which motivates reporting uncertainty for the gain over simple baselines.\n648\t\n649\t\\paragraph{Benchmarks.} The data comprise per-item outcomes on HELM-derived and Open LLM Leaderboard-derived benchmarks \\citep{Liang2022HolisticEO}: development benchmarks IFEval \\citep{Zhou2023InstructionFollowingEF}, ARC-Challenge \\citep{Clark2018ThinkYH}, MuSR \\citep{Sprague2023MuSRTT}, GSM8K \\citep{Cobbe2021TrainingVT} and BBH \\citep{Suzgun2022ChallengingBT}; and held-out benchmarks GPQA \\citep{Rein2023GPQAAG}, MedQA \\citep{Jin2020WhatDD}, LegalBench \\citep{Guha2023LegalBenchAC}, MMLU \\citep{Hendrycks2020MeasuringMM} and MMLU-Pro \\citep{Wang2024MMLUProAM}.\n650\t\n651\t\\section{Setting and Method}\n652\t\\label{sec:method}\n653\t\\subsection{Problem setup}\n654\tFor a benchmark with $n$ items, let $S\\in\\{0,1\\}^{m\\times n}$ be the binary correctness matrix of $m$ source models. A \\emph{target} model is a new model whose per-item results are hidden except on a chosen subset. The procedure has two steps. A \\emph{selector} sees only $S$ (with opaque item indices) and returns $k{=}100$ distinct item indices. The evaluator then reveals the targets' answers $Y\\in\\{0,1\\}^{t\\times k}$ on those items only, and a \\emph{predictor} returns a predicted full-benchmark accuracy $\\hat y_j$ for each target $j$. The reference is the target's true full accuracy $y_j$. Per episode we compute MAE, RMSE, Spearman and Kendall correlation between $\\hat y$ and $y$ across the targets of that episode. Targets are drawn in one of two regimes: \\emph{random targets} (a random 20\\% of models, sources are the rest) and \\emph{strong targets} (targets drawn from models whose full accuracy is at or above the 0.7 quantile; sources are the models strictly below it, so every target exceeds every source). The regime label is supplied to the candidate estimator with the source data; the baseline functions in the evaluator take only the source matrix and the target responses on their chosen items (\\texttt{source\\_evidence/evaluate\\_final.py}, baseline functions).\n655\t\n656\t\\subsection{Design rationale}\n657\tThe configuration is motivated by three observations; we state them as motivation, not as demonstrated mechanisms, because no single-factor ablation was run (Section~\\ref{sec:discussion}).\n658\t(i) Binary outcomes saturate: a linear latent model can predict probabilities outside $[0,1]$ and must be clipped, whereas a logistic model keeps item probabilities calibrated and naturally describes how an item discriminates between abilities.\n659\t(ii) Under a logistic model an item's contribution to the information about a target's ability is $p(1-p)\\,a a^{\\top}$, which is largest near $p=0.5$. Evaluating this at reference abilities in the \\emph{upper tail} of the source abilities, rather than at the centre, favours items that remain informative for strong targets.\n660\t(iii) Every hyperparameter and the estimator weights must be chosen without target information. Pseudo-targets inside the source set provide this: random folds mimic in-range targets, while holding out the strongest source models mimics out-of-range targets.\n661\t\n662\t\\subsection{Selected estimator}\n663\tImplementation details below are taken from the readable source of the evaluated configuration (\\texttt{source\\_evidence/candidate\\_program.py}; the extension of Table~\\ref{tab:search} is in \\texttt{source\\_evidence/extension\\_program.py}).\n664\t\\paragraph{Latent model.} A rank-$r$ logistic model, $\\operatorname{logit}P_{mi}=b_i+a_i^{\\top}\\theta_m$, is fitted to $S$ by alternating L2-regularized Newton sweeps over item parameters $(b_i,a_i)$ and source abilities $\\theta_m$, initialized from the SVD of the centred matrix. The empirical mean and covariance of the source abilities define a Gaussian prior for target abilities. A linear SVD-based latent model (item means, loadings, per-item residual variance, ridge/MAP ability) is fitted as well and serves as a stack member.\n665\t\n666\t\\paragraph{Item design.} Candidate designs of exactly $k$ items are generated: difficulty-stratified selection, a greedy Bayesian D-optimal design over linear-model item loadings, hybrids with a $k$-means medoid half, and three Fisher designs under the logistic model (pure, half stratified, half linear D-optimal). The Fisher design greedily maximizes $\\log\\det$ of $\\tau\\,\\Sigma_\\theta^{-1}+\\sum_{i\\in\\text{design}} \\bar w_i a_i a_i^{\\top}$, with $\\bar w_i$ the mean of $p_i(1-p_i)$ over reference abilities taken from the top 10\\% of source abilities along the direction of increasing accuracy, shifted by $\\{0,0.5,1\\}$ standard deviations beyond it.\n667\t\n668\t\\paragraph{Target estimate.} For a target with answers $Y$ on items $\\mathcal I$, ability is the MAP estimate under the Gaussian prior (Newton iterations on the $k$ answers). The model-assisted estimate, with and without a residual correction, is\n669\t\\[\n670\t\\hat y_{\\text{plain}} = \\frac1n\\Big(\\sum_{i\\in\\mathcal I} Y_i+\\sum_{i\\notin\\mathcal I}\\hat p_i\\Big),\\qquad\n671\t\\hat y_{\\text{corr}} = \\hat y_{\\text{plain}} + \\frac{n-k}{n}\\,\\overline{(Y-\\hat p)_{\\mathcal I}},\n672\t\\]\n673\twhere $\\hat p_i$ is the model probability and the correction adds the mean residual of the revealed items, extrapolated to the unrevealed items. A third estimator is the \\emph{calibrated mean}: a linear map, fitted on source models, from subset mean to full accuracy.\n674\t\n675\t\\paragraph{Source-validated stacking.} For each design, rank, prior scale and residual-correction choice, predictions are made on source pseudo-targets: five random folds for random targets, or two folds that hold out the strongest 20\\% and 10\\% of sources for strong targets. Non-negative least squares with a heavily weighted sum-to-one row gives weights (summing to approximately one) over the calibrated mean, the linear estimate and the logistic estimate. Among the fifteen configurations with lowest pseudo-target error, the one minimizing a normalized RMSE plus rank-loss objective is retained. The calibrated mean on a stratified design is always in the search space, and any numerical failure or time-out of the logistic fit falls back to the simpler configuration. No target information enters selection or weights.\n676\t\n677\t\\subsection{Compared approaches}\n678\t\\textbf{Baselines} (evaluator-owned re-implementations, same $k=100$, same episodes): \\emph{random mean}, the target's mean over $k$ random items; \\emph{anchor points}, $k$-means with $k$ clusters on item response columns of the source matrix, medoid anchors weighted by cluster size; and \\emph{IRT-style}, a three-dimensional logistic IRT model fitted by Adam (200 iterations; \\texttt{evaluate\\_final.py}, \\texttt{IRT\\_DIMS}, \\texttt{IRT\\_ITERS}), $k$-means on item parameters to pick anchors, a ridge-regularized maximum-likelihood ability, and the mean predicted probability over all items. A fourth reference predicts the constant source mean and is used only inside the negative controls.\n679\t\n680\t\\textbf{Within-family variants} (development benchmarks only): a linear model-assisted D-optimal estimator without the logistic model or Fisher design (the stack's fallback member), the selected stacked configuration, and an extension that adds bagged posterior-mean probabilities and a tail-calibration column to the stack. These differ by cumulative changes, not single factors, and are reported as such.\n681\t\n682\t\\section{Experimental Protocol}\n683\t\\label{sec:protocol}\n684\t\\paragraph{Data.} Binary per-item outcome matrices (models $\\times$ items) come from the public per-item score release accompanying \\citet{Zhang2025HowBP}, built from the Open LLM Leaderboard and HELM. Five benchmarks were available for development (IFEval, ARC-Challenge, MuSR, GSM8K, BBH) and five disjoint benchmarks were reserved for the sealed final evaluation (GPQA, MedQA, LegalBench, MMLU, MMLU-Pro). The development evaluation uses seeds 11 and 12 (22 episodes including two controls); the final evaluation uses seeds 9101--9103 (32 episodes including two controls). Item order is randomly permuted per benchmark and seed. The target count is the larger of 5 and 20\\% of the models; every episode requires at least 40 sources.\n685\t\n686\t\\paragraph{Utility score and aggregation.} Per episode, utility is $u=0.5\\,\\mathrm{clip}(1-2\\,\\mathrm{MAE},0,1)+0.5\\,(\\rho+1)/2$ with $\\rho$ the Spearman correlation across targets. Episodes are averaged over seeds within each (benchmark, regime), then over benchmarks with equal weight, then over regimes with weights $0.5/0.5$. MAE, RMSE, Spearman and Kendall in the tables use the same hierarchy. Gains over baselines use the sign convention that positive favours the candidate: $\\Delta\\mathrm{MAE}=\\mathrm{MAE}_{\\text{baseline}}-\\mathrm{MAE}_{\\text{cand}}$ and $\\Delta u=u_{\\text{cand}}-u_{\\text{baseline}}$. The best baseline is the one with the highest utility; this is the random-subset mean, so gains against ``the best baseline'' and against the random mean coincide.\n687\t\n688\t\\paragraph{Uncertainty.} For the gain over the best baseline we report a paired hierarchical bootstrap (300 replicates; resampling benchmarks, then seeds within benchmark, then target models within episode; 95\\% percentile interval; bootstrap seed 20260101; \\texttt{source\\_evidence/evaluate\\_final.py}, \\texttt{bootstrap\\_deltas}). Only five benchmarks and three seeds are resampled, so the interval is coarse. No interval is available for the anchor-point and IRT-style gains, and baseline-specific Spearman values are not part of the retained outputs; we do not infer significance for those comparisons.\n689\t\n690\t\\paragraph{Controls and qualification.} Two negative controls replace the target responses on the selected items: a permutation that assigns each target another target's answers, and a constant response pattern equal to the source majority. The final evaluation is labeled \\emph{qualified} by the evaluator, with completed status and 32 of 32 episodes completed. We read this label as a statement about the evaluation outcome only, not as evidence of superiority or generalization. The evidence files mark the candidate-side diagnostics as unverified and provide no gate-level diagnostics, so we do not state which criteria underlie the label and attribute nothing to any individual criterion.\n691\t\n692\t\\section{Results}\n693\t\\label{sec:results}\n694\t\\subsection{Held-out comparison with baselines}\n695\tTable~\\ref{tab:final} and Figure~\\ref{fig:main} report the sealed evaluation on the five held-out benchmarks. All 32 episodes completed and none failed. The stacked configuration reaches MAE 0.0294, RMSE 0.0354, Spearman 0.843 and Kendall 0.715 (utility 0.9314). \n696\tBaseline MAEs, derived as candidate MAE plus the reported gain, are 0.0339 (random mean), 0.0428 (anchor points) and 0.0692 (IRT-style); the corresponding point-estimate gains are $+0.0045$, $+0.0134$ and $+0.0398$. \n697\tThe utility gain over the best baseline is $+0.0093$. The point estimates therefore favour the candidate over all three baselines.\n698\t\n699\tThe interval, however, does not resolve the comparison that matters most. The 95\\% bootstrap interval for the MAE gain over the random-subset mean is $[-0.0052,+0.0134]$ and for the utility gain $[-0.0143,+0.0354]$; both contain zero. \n700\tWe therefore describe the gain as a modest point-estimate improvement that is not statistically resolved with five benchmarks and three seeds. The larger gains over anchor points and the IRT-style estimator cannot be assessed for significance with the retained outputs. The IRT-style baseline is a fixed-configuration re-implementation (three latent dimensions, 200 optimizer iterations); its weak result should not be read as a verdict on IRT-based prediction in general.\n701\t\n702\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n703\t\\centering\n704\t\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/main_comparison.pdf}\n705\t\\caption{Held-out final evaluation (five benchmarks, three seeds, $k{=}100$). (A) Overall MAE of the stacked configuration and three baselines; baseline bars (hatched) are derived as candidate MAE plus the evaluator-reported gain. (B) Paired gain over the best baseline (the random-subset mean) in MAE and utility, with 95\\% hierarchical bootstrap intervals; both intervals include zero.}\n706\t\\label{fig:main}\n707\t\\end{figure}\n708\t\n709\t\\begin{table}[t]\n710\t\\centering\n711\t\\caption{Sealed final evaluation on held-out benchmarks (five benchmarks, seeds 9101--9103, $k{=}100$). Gains are positive when the stacked configuration is better. ``Derived'' baseline MAEs equal candidate MAE plus the reported gain.}\n712\t\\label{tab:final}\n713\t\\small\n714\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n715\t\\toprule"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c7e94be0-999b-4a01-9c75-8343542544f0","uuid":"d6c571cd-da0f-425d-8e5e-9ae1d761aa79","timestamp":"2026-11-11T10:57:41.603Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/workspace/paper_evidence_audit_packet.md","content":"# Agentic evidence audit packet\n\n- generated_at: `2026-11-11T10:57:37.596650+00:00`\n- paper_dir: `work/results/t07/claude/20261111_091120/.paper_evidence_audit_workspaces-1000/agentic-evidence-audit-1000-20261111_101106-paper_evidence_audit_round_4-kse_2nxd/workspace`\n\nUse this packet as the default entrypoint. Read raw artifacts only when this packet is insufficient.\nThe goal is narrow: check whether the paper's visible claims remain consistent with claims_grounding.json, paper_data.json, and the scientific-evidence trail.\n\n## Expected audit focus\n\n- visible claims in `paper.tex` that overstate, contradict, or drift from `claims_grounding.json`\n- claims grounded to the wrong source ids, or source ids that do not support the visible wording\n- mismatches between visible manuscript text and the resolved/anchored evidence trail\n- cases where the evidence trail exists but does not justify the paper's wording or numeric precision\n- co