{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019CA9oSMf6bRUvpv7tyceGK","type":"tool_result","content":"128\t\\caption{Sealed final evaluation on held-out benchmarks (five benchmarks, seeds 9101--9103, $k{=}100$). Gains are positive when the stacked configuration is better. ``Derived'' baseline MAEs equal candidate MAE plus the reported gain.}\n129\t\\label{tab:final}\n130\t\\small\n131\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n132\t\\toprule\n133\tQuantity & Value & 95\\% CI \\\\\n134\t\\midrule\n135\tMAE / RMSE & 0.0294 / 0.0354 & --- \\\\ % @evidence[[derived/metrics_summary.json#final.rmse]]\n136\tSpearman / Kendall & 0.843 / 0.715 & --- \\\\\n137\tUtility & 0.9314 & --- \\\\\n138\tUtility per seed (9101 / 9102 / 9103) & 0.9257 / 0.9324 / 0.9360 & --- \\\\ % @evidence[[derived/metrics_summary.json#final.per_seed_utility]]\n139\tMAE, random / strong targets & 0.0179 / 0.0409 & --- \\\\\n140\tSpearman, random / strong targets & 0.956 / 0.730 & --- \\\\\n141\t\\midrule\n142\tMAE gain vs.\\ random mean (best baseline) & $+0.0045$ & $[-0.0052,+0.0134]$ \\\\\n143\tUtility gain vs.\\ best baseline & $+0.0093$ & $[-0.0143,+0.0354]$ \\\\\n144\tMAE gain vs.\\ anchor points & $+0.0134$ & not available \\\\\n145\tMAE gain vs.\\ IRT-style & $+0.0398$ & not available \\\\\n146\t\\midrule\n147\tPermuted: Spearman drop / MAE rise & 1.157 / 0.156 & --- \\\\ % @evidence[[derived/metrics_summary.json#final.control_permuted_mae_increase]]\n148\tConstant: MAE rise & 0.094 & --- \\\\\n149\tEpisodes completed / failed & 32 / 0 & --- \\\\\n150\t\\bottomrule\n151\t\\end{tabular}\n152\t\\end{table}\n153\t\n154\t\\subsection{Extrapolation to targets stronger than all sources}\n155\tFigure~\\ref{fig:gap} separates the two regimes. On the held-out benchmarks, MAE is 0.0179 for random targets and 0.0409 for strong targets, a factor of about 2.3, and Spearman correlation falls from 0.956 to 0.730. % @evidence[[derived/metrics_summary.json#final.spearman_strong_targets]]\n156\tOn the development benchmarks, the selected configuration shows the same pattern: MAE 0.0185 versus 0.0359 and Spearman 0.963 versus 0.816. % @evidence[[derived/metrics_summary.json#search.c02.mae_strong_targets]]\n157\tThe gap is therefore not an artefact of one benchmark group, although it is larger on the held-out group. Even with a design aimed at upper-tail abilities and pseudo-target folds that hold out the strongest sources, strong-target ranking is much worse than random-target ranking; the configuration does not solve extrapolation. Because per-regime baseline results are not part of the retained outputs, we cannot say whether the gain over baselines is concentrated in one regime, nor whether baselines suffer a similar gap. Part of the regime difference may reflect statistical structure rather than extrapolation as such: strong targets occupy a narrower range of true scores, which by itself lowers rank correlation for a given prediction error. We did not test this.\n158\t\n159\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n160\t\\centering\n161\t\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/extrapolation_gap.pdf}\n162\t\\caption{Error and rank correlation by target regime for the selected stacked configuration. Development benchmarks (search evaluation, two seeds) and held-out benchmarks (final evaluation, three seeds) are different benchmark sets and are never pooled. (A) MAE. (B) Spearman correlation across targets.}\n163\t\\label{fig:gap}\n164\t\\end{figure}\n165\t\n166\t\\subsection{Negative controls}\n167\tPermuting target answers across targets raises MAE by 0.156 and lowers Spearman by 1.157 relative to the matched real episode, and replacing answers by a constant pattern raises MAE by 0.094. % @evidence[[derived/metrics_summary.json#final.control_constant_mae_delta]]\n168\tPredictions therefore depend on the target's revealed answers, as one would require of a valid estimator. The controls do not measure accuracy. Each control is a single episode matched to one real random-target episode, on one benchmark (MMLU-Pro) and the first seed of the final evaluation (\\texttt{source\\_evidence/evaluate\\_final.py}, episode plan), so the control deltas carry no benchmark or seed variance.\n169\t\n170\t\\subsection{Within-family variants on development benchmarks}\n171\tTable~\\ref{tab:search} compares the three configurations of the family on the development benchmarks (two seeds each; all three were scored, none failed). The utility is 0.9381 for the linear model-assisted estimator, 0.9452 for the selected stack and 0.9445 for the stack with bagged posterior means and tail calibration. % @evidence[[derived/metrics_summary.json#search.c02.combined_score]]\n172\tThe difference between the latter two (0.0006) is smaller than the seed-to-seed spread inside a variant (for the selected stack, 0.9423 versus 0.9480), so the choice of the selected stack over its extension is a frozen choice, not a demonstrated superiority. The extension, which targets strong-target error, did not improve it (strong-target MAE 0.0362 versus 0.0359; Spearman 0.812 versus 0.816), which is consistent with the interpretation that additional tail correction fitted on source pseudo-targets adds nothing measurable here. The gain from the linear estimator to the stack (+0.0071 utility) is larger than the spread but arises from three simultaneous changes (logistic model, Fisher design, stacking) and two seeds, so it cannot be attributed to any component.\n173\t\n174\t\\begin{table}[t]\n175\t\\centering\n176\t\\caption{Development benchmarks (five benchmarks, seeds 11 and 12, $k{=}100$): three configurations of the same estimator family. The first row is the linear model-assisted estimator; the second is the configuration carried to the held-out evaluation; the third adds bagged posterior means and tail calibration. Utility is the aggregate defined in Section~\\ref{sec:protocol}. Full metrics are in Table~\\ref{tab:search-full}.}\n177\t\\label{tab:search}\n178\t\\small\n179\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n180\t\\toprule\n181\tConfiguration & Utility & Per-seed utility (11 / 12) & MAE rand / strong & Spearman strong \\\\\n182\t\\midrule\n183\tLinear model-assisted & 0.9381 & 0.9369 / 0.9393 & 0.0180 / 0.0389 & 0.771 \\\\\n184\tStack (selected) & 0.9452 & 0.9423 / 0.9480 & 0.0185 / 0.0359 & 0.816 \\\\\n185\tStack + tail calibration & 0.9445 & 0.9423 / 0.9468 & 0.0185 / 0.0362 & 0.812 \\\\\n186\t\\bottomrule\n187\t\\end{tabular}\n188\t\\end{table}\n189\t\n190\t\\subsection{Search versus held-out performance}\n191\tThe selected configuration's utility is 0.9452 on the development benchmarks and 0.9314 on the held-out benchmarks. The benchmark sets, seeds, and number of seeds differ, so we report the difference without a causal explanation. The MAE gain over the random mean is also larger on development benchmarks ($+0.0086$) than on held-out benchmarks ($+0.0045$). % @evidence[[derived/metrics_summary.json#search.c02.delta_mae_vs_random_mean]]\n192\tDevelopment scores are proxies that influenced which configuration was frozen; held-out scores are the appropriate basis for any claim.\n193\t\n194\t\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n195\t\\label{sec:discussion}\n196\t\\paragraph{What the evidence supports.} The stacked configuration has lower point-estimate MAE than three matched baselines on five held-out benchmarks, predictions that degrade as expected under both negative controls (each a single episode; no pass/fail criterion for the controls is evidenced), and shows a large degradation for stronger-than-source targets. It does not establish a statistically resolved improvement over a random-subset mean, a superiority claim, or generalization to other benchmarks, budgets or score types. The evaluator's ``qualified'' label is not a superiority or generalization claim, and no gate-level diagnostics are available to interpret it further.\n197\t\n198\t\\paragraph{Attribution.} The configuration differs from the random-mean baseline in four ways at once: items are selected using source data, a source-fitted calibration maps subset means to full accuracy, latent-model predictions are blended in, and the regime label selects the validation folds. No experiment isolates these. The gain over random mean could come from calibration alone, from item selection, or from the latent models; the benefit of the Fisher design under upper-tail reference abilities, in particular, is untested. The regime label is not given to the baselines, so the candidate has information they lack; this should be kept in mind when reading the comparison.\n199\t\n200\t\\paragraph{Threats to validity.} (i) Five benchmarks and three seeds make the bootstrap coarse; a larger panel might show a clear gain or none. (ii) Targets within an episode are few for some benchmarks, so Spearman and Kendall are noisy per episode. (iii) The strong-target regime is one definition (above the 0.7 accuracy quantile, sources below it) and need not represent real future models. (iv) Baselines are the evaluator's re-implementations with fixed settings; stronger tuned versions might narrow the gap. (v) Selection of the configuration used development scores from two seeds with within-variant spread comparable to between-variant differences. (vi) Candidate diagnostics are marked unverified.\n201\t\n202\t\\paragraph{Practical reading.} For a user who only needs coarse model scores on benchmarks resembling the historical pool, the random-target numbers (MAE 0.0179, Spearman 0.956 at $k=100$) indicate that 100 items can recover full accuracy to within about two percentage points on average and keep rankings largely intact. % @evidence[[derived/metrics_summary.json#final.mae_random_targets]]\n203\tFor a user who must rank systems that exceed everything in the historical pool, the strong-target numbers (MAE 0.0409, Spearman 0.730) indicate that subset predictions alone should be treated as screening information, with direct evaluation reserved for the decisions that matter. Whether the stacked configuration or a plain random-subset mean is preferable for this purpose cannot be decided from our evidence, because their difference is smaller than the uncertainty we measured; the random mean is simpler, needs no source data, and has no regime-dependent tuning.\n204\t\n205\t\\paragraph{Relation to published numbers.} We do not compare against numbers reported in earlier papers: protocols differ in benchmark sets, target definitions, item budgets and metrics, and our baselines are re-implementations with fixed settings rather than the original code. Conclusions here are limited to the matched comparison on the evaluator's episodes.\n206\t\n207\t\\paragraph{What would resolve the comparison.} The width of the MAE-gain interval (0.0186) is about four times its point estimate (0.0045), so the held-out panel is too small to distinguish a gain of this size from zero. A decisive test would need more benchmarks and seeds than the five and three used here, paired in the same way, together with per-regime baseline results so that any gain can be located in the in-range or out-of-range regime. We expect, but have not verified, that the sign of the gain is more stable than its magnitude.\n208\t\n209\t\\paragraph{Not tested.} A sweep over the item budget $k$, additional seeds, single-factor ablations (latent rank, prior shrinkage, logistic versus linear model, Fisher versus stratified design, stacking versus a single estimator), temporal or model-family splits, and non-binary scores were not run. These are the experiments needed to attribute any gain to a mechanism.\n210\t\n211\t\\section{Conclusion}\n212\tWe evaluated a source-validated stacked latent-ability estimator with Fisher D-optimal item design for predicting a new model's full-benchmark accuracy from 100 items. On five held-out benchmarks it had the lowest point-estimate MAE among the compared estimators (0.0294 versus 0.0339 for the random-subset mean) and its predictions degraded as expected under both negative controls (single episodes; no pass/fail criterion is evidenced), but the interval for its gain over the best baseline spans zero, so the improvement is not statistically established. The largest measured difference is that targets stronger than every source model are predicted about twice as badly (MAE 0.0409, Spearman 0.730) as random targets (MAE 0.0179, Spearman 0.956), on both development and held-out benchmark groups (point estimates, without intervals, and possibly partly due to the narrower score range of strong targets), and the design aimed at upper-tail abilities did not close this gap. Practically, a user who needs to rank models stronger than the historical pool should not rely on source-matrix-based subset prediction without direct validation on a few full evaluations. Future work should test a budget sweep, single-factor ablations and per-regime baseline comparisons to learn which component, if any, helps, and whether item selection can be made robust for out-of-range targets.\n213\t\n214\t% IMPORTANT: \\clearpage flushes all pending floats before references so that\n215\t% body-page counting excludes reference-only pages.  Do NOT remove.\n216\t\\clearpage\n217\t\\bibliography{references}\n218\t\\bibliographystyle{paper}\n219\t\n220\t\\clearpage\n221\t\\appendix\n222\t\\section{Supplementary Information}\n223\t\n224\t\\subsection{Evaluation protocol and metric definitions}\n225\tEach episode is identified by a seed, a benchmark and a regime. For random targets the evaluator draws as targets 20\\% of the $M$ models with results on the benchmark (rounded to the nearest integer, at least 5) and uses the rest as sources. For strong targets, the pool is the set of models with full accuracy at or above the 0.7 quantile; sources are models strictly below it, and the targets are drawn from the pool (at most the same number). Episodes require at least 40 sources and 5 targets. The source matrix is transferred in at most two chunks of bit-packed rows (\\texttt{source\\_evidence/evaluate\\_final.py}, \\texttt{MAX\\_LOAD\\_CHUNKS}, \\texttt{pack\\_bits}). The selector must return exactly $k{=}100$ distinct valid indices, and the predictor must return one finite prediction per target. Aggregation proceeds from seeds to benchmarks (equal weight) to regimes (0.5/0.5); failed or missing episodes would contribute worst-case values, but none occurred in either evaluation (22 and 32 episodes completed, 0 failed). The development benchmarks are IFEval, ARC-Challenge, MuSR, GSM8K and BBH; the held-out benchmarks are GPQA, MedQA, LegalBench, MMLU and MMLU-Pro. The number of observed candidate calls was 88 in the development evaluation and 128 in the final evaluation.\n226\t\n227\t\\subsection{Estimator details of the selected configuration}\n228\t\\begin{itemize}\n229\t\\item \\textbf{Linear latent model:} centred source matrix, SVD of the $m\\times m$ Gram matrix, maximum rank 12; item residual variances are floored at $\\max(0.02,\\text{5th percentile})$. Rank grid $\\{1,2,3,5,8,12\\}$ and prior-scale grid $\\tau\\in\\{1,4,16\\}$.\n230\t\\item \\textbf{Logistic latent model:} eight alternating Newton sweeps with L2 penalties 0.5 (loadings), 0.05 (intercepts) and 1.0 (abilities); steps clipped to $[-2,2]$; rank grid $\\{2,3,1,5\\}$ evaluated in that order under a 45-second budget for logistic fitting, after which remaining ranks are skipped; prior-scale grid $\\{1,4,16\\}$; target abilities by up to 15 Newton iterations.\n231\t\\item \\textbf{Designs:} four linear designs (stratified; stratified$+$D-optimal half; D-optimal; $k$-means$+$D-optimal half) and three Fisher designs (pure; half stratified; half linear D-optimal), the latter built at logistic rank 3 with reference abilities from the top 10\\% of source abilities plus margins $\\{0,0.5,1.0\\}$ standard deviations.\n232\t\\item \\textbf{Blend grid:} linear or logistic estimator blended with the calibrated mean with weights $\\{0,0.2,\\dots,1\\}$, plus NNLS stacks over the three best linear and three best logistic candidates per design.\n233\t\\item \\textbf{Selection objective:} among the 15 lowest-MSE pseudo-target configurations, minimize $\\mathrm{RMSE}/\\mathrm{RMSE}_{\\min}+(1-\\overline{(\\rho+\\tau)/2})$, with the rank term averaged over folds.\n234\t\\item \\textbf{Fallbacks:} if validation cannot run (fewer than ten source models) a conservative blend of 0.7 calibrated mean and 0.3 linear estimate on a hybrid design is used; any exception returns a stratified design with the calibrated mean.\n235\t\\end{itemize}\n236\tThe values above are constants and routines in \\texttt{source\\_evidence/candidate\\_program.py} (constant block and the functions \\texttt{fit\\_logistic\\_latent}, \\texttt{\\_make\\_folds}, \\texttt{\\_validate}, \\texttt{stack\\_weights}). The extension in Table~\\ref{tab:search} (\\texttt{source\\_evidence/extension\\_program.py}) additionally uses bagged logistic fits at ranks 2, 3 and 4, a Laplace posterior mean for item probabilities, and a ridge-shrunk monotone tail correction applied only above the source median accuracy, accepted only if it beats the incumbent on pseudo-targets.\n237\t\n238\t\\subsection{Development-benchmark results for all three configurations}\n239\t\\begin{table}[h]\n240\t\\centering\n241\t\\caption{Complete development-benchmark metrics (five benchmarks, seeds 11 and 12). Gains are positive when the configuration is better than the baseline. Rows: linear model-assisted estimator; selected stack; stack with tail calibration.}\n242\t\\label{tab:search-full}\n243\t\\footnotesize\n244\t\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{3pt}\n245\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n246\t\\toprule\n247\tMetric & Linear & Stack (selected) & Stack + tail \\\\\n248\t\\midrule\n249\tUtility & 0.9381 & 0.9452 & 0.9445 \\\\ % @evidence[[derived/metrics_summary.json#search.c01.combined_score]]\n250\tMAE / RMSE & 0.0285 / 0.0347 & 0.0272 / 0.0336 & 0.0274 / 0.0339 \\\\\n251\tSpearman / Kendall & 0.866 / 0.730 & 0.889 / 0.756 & 0.888 / 0.752 \\\\\n252\tMAE random / strong & 0.0180 / 0.0389 & 0.0185 / 0.0359 & 0.0185 / 0.0362 \\\\\n253\tSpearman random / strong & 0.962 / 0.771 & 0.963 / 0.816 & 0.963 / 0.812 \\\\\n254\tMAE gain vs.\\ random mean & $+0.0073$ & $+0.0086$ & $+0.0084$ \\\\\n255\tMAE gain vs.\\ anchor points & $+0.0096$ & $+0.0109$ & $+0.0107$ \\\\\n256\tMAE gain vs.\\ IRT-style & $+0.0405$ & $+0.0418$ & $+0.0416$ \\\\\n257\tUtility gain vs.\\ best baseline & $+0.0084$ & $+0.0155$ & $+0.0148$ \\\\\n258\tPermuted: Spearman drop / MAE increase & 1.021 / 0.109 & 1.033 / 0.110 & 1.033 / 0.110 \\\\\n259\tConstant: MAE increase & 0.077 & 0.077 & 0.077 \\\\\n260\t\\bottomrule\n261\t\\end{tabular}\n262\t\\end{table}\n263\t\n264\t\\subsection{Final evaluation details}\n265\tThe per-seed utilities of the selected configuration are 0.9257 (seed 9101), 0.9324 (seed 9102) and 0.9360 (seed 9103); their spread (0.0103) is comparable to the point-estimate utility gain over the best baseline (0.0093), consistent with the unresolved bootstrap interval. % @evidence[[derived/metrics_summary.json#final.per_seed_utility]]\n266\tRecorded wall-clock time per seeded run was 150.8, 77.4 and 75.1 seconds respectively for the three seeds. The evaluator reports 128 candidate calls observed over 32 episodes. Utility differences against anchor points and IRT-style baselines are not accompanied by intervals in the retained outputs. The evaluation was declared \\emph{qualified} with completed status; candidate-side diagnostics are recorded as unverified.\n267\t\n268\t\\subsection{Reproducibility, data provenance and seeds}\n269\t\\textbf{Data.} The per-item matrices are the public score files released with \\citet{Zhang2025HowBP}, pinned to a fixed repository commit (55faff49\\ldots{}), derived from the Open LLM Leaderboard and HELM. \\textbf{Seeds.} Development 11, 12; final 9101, 9102, 9103; bootstrap 20260101; the evaluated configuration seeds its own generators from the episode seed. \\textbf{Software.} The task requirements file (\\texttt{source\\_evidence/requirements.txt}) pins numpy 2.5.4, scipy 1.18.1, scikit-learn 1.9.1 and joblib 1.6.0; the evaluated configuration sets eight BLAS threads by default (\\texttt{candidate\\_program.py}, header). \\textbf{Figures.} Figures~\\ref{fig:main} and \\ref{fig:gap} are produced by a single plotting script from the evaluator outputs and the retained metrics files; the overview figure is a schematic and contains no data. \\textbf{Code.} The numbers derive from a single-file estimator and the evaluator script, whose baselines are the evaluator's own implementations; copies of the sources are supplied under \\texttt{source\\_evidence/} and are not linked in this anonymized draft. We did not access the raw leaderboard details, which are partly gated.\n270\t\n271\t\\subsection{LLM Usage}\n272\tLarge language models were used throughout this work. An LLM-based research agent proposed the estimator variants, implemented them, ran the development and final evaluations through the provided harness, selected related work from a starter list and retrieved references, and drafted this manuscript. All reported numbers originate from the evaluator outputs and retained metric files, not from model generation; the LLM did not generate data. The agent did not access any content excluded by the task rules.\n273\t\n274\t\\end{document}\n275\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c7e94be0-999b-4a01-9c75-8343542544f0","uuid":"898944bc-c30b-4784-b189-2d74c780d6c8","timestamp":"2026-11-11T10:57:46.686Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/workspace/paper.tex","content":"\\caption{Sealed final evaluation on held-out benchmarks (five benchmarks, seeds 9101--9103, $k{=}100$). Gains are positive when the stacked configuration is better. ``Derived'' baseline MAEs equal candidate MAE plus the reported gain.}\n\\label{tab:final}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n\\toprule\nQuantity & Value & 95\\% CI \\\\\n\\midrule\nMAE / RMSE & 0.0294 / 0.0354 & --- \\\\ % @evidence[[derived/metrics_summary.json#final.rmse]]\nSpearman / Kendall & 0.843 / 0.715 & --- \\\\\nUtility & 0.9314 & --- \\\\\nUtility per seed (9101 / 9102 / 9103) & 0.9257 / 0.9324 / 0.9360 & --- \\\\ % @evidence[[derived/metrics_summary.json#final.per_seed_utility]]\nMAE, random / strong targets & 0.0179 / 0.0409 & --- \\\\\nSpearman, random / strong targets & 0.956 / 0.730 & --- \\\\\n\\midrule\nMAE gain vs.\\ random mean (best baseline) & $+0.0045$ & $[-0.0052,+0.0134]$ \\\\\nUtility gain vs.\\ best baseline & $+0.0093$ & $[-0.0143,+0.0354]$ \\\\\nMAE gain vs.\\ anchor points & $+0.0134$ & not available \\\\\nMAE gain vs.\\ IRT-style & $+0.0398$ & not available \\\\\n\\midrule\nPermuted: Spearman drop / MAE rise & 1.157 / 0.156 & --- \\\\ % @evidence[[derived/metrics_summary.json#final.control_permuted_mae_increase]]\nConstant: MAE rise & 0.094 & --- \\\\\nEpisodes completed / failed & 32 / 0 & --- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Extrapolation to targets stronger than all sources}\nFigure~\\ref{fig:gap} separates the two regimes. On the held-out benchmarks, MAE is 0.0179 for random targets and 0.0409 for strong targets, a factor of about 2.3, and Spearman correlation falls from 0.956 to 0.730. % @evidence[[derived/metrics_summary.json#final.spearman_strong_targets]]\nOn the development benchmarks, the selected configuration shows the same pattern: MAE 0.0185 versus 0.0359 and Spearman 0.963 versus 0.816. % @evidence[[derived/metrics_summary.json#search.c02.mae_strong_targets]]\nThe gap is therefore not an artefact of one benchmark group, although it is larger on the held-out group. Even with a design aimed at upper-tail abilities and pseudo-target folds that hold out the strongest sources, strong-target ranking is much worse than random-target ranking; the configuration does not solve extrapolation. Because per-regime baseline results are not part of the retained outputs, we cannot say whether the gain over baselines is concentrated in one regime, nor whether baselines suffer a similar gap. Part of the regime difference may reflect statistical structure rather than extrapolation as such: strong targets occupy a narrower range of true scores, which by itself lowers rank correlation for a given prediction error. We did not test this.\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/extrapolation_gap.pdf}\n\\caption{Error and rank correlation by target regime for the selected stacked configuration. Development benchmarks (search evaluation, two seeds) and held-out benchmarks (final evaluation, three seeds) are different benchmark sets and are never pooled. (A) MAE. (B) Spearman correlation across targets.}\n\\label{fig:gap}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\subsection{Negative controls}\nPermuting target answers across targets raises MAE by 0.156 and lowers Spearman by 1.157 relative to the matched real episode, and replacing answers by a constant pattern raises MAE by 0.094. % @evidence[[derived/metrics_summary.json#final.control_constant_mae_delta]]\nPredictions therefore depend on the target's revealed answers, as one would require of a valid estimator. The controls do not measure accuracy. Each control is a single episode matched to one real random-target episode, on one benchmark (MMLU-Pro) and the first seed of the final evaluation (\\texttt{source\\_evidence/evaluate\\_final.py}, episode plan), so the control deltas carry no benchmark or seed variance.\n\n\\subsection{Within-family variants on development benchmarks}\nTable~\\ref{tab:search} compares the three configurations of the family on the development benchmarks (two seeds each; all three were scored, none failed). The utility is 0.9381 for the linear model-assisted estimator, 0.9452 for the selected stack and 0.9445 for the stack with bagged posterior means and tail calibration. % @evidence[[derived/metrics_summary.json#search.c02.combined_score]]\nThe difference between the latter two (0.0006) is smaller than the seed-to-seed spread inside a variant (for the selected stack, 0.9423 versus 0.9480), so the choice of the selected stack over its extension is a frozen choice, not a demonstrated superiority. The extension, which targets strong-target error, did not improve it (strong-target MAE 0.0362 versus 0.0359; Spearman 0.812 versus 0.816), which is consistent with the interpretation that additional tail correction fitted on source pseudo-targets adds nothing measurable here. The gain from the linear estimator to the stack (+0.0071 utility) is larger than the spread but arises from three simultaneous changes (logistic model, Fisher design, stacking) and two seeds, so it cannot be attributed to any component.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Development benchmarks (five benchmarks, seeds 11 and 12, $k{=}100$): three configurations of the same estimator family. The first row is the linear model-assisted estimator; the second is the configuration carried to the held-out evaluation; the third adds bagged posterior means and tail calibration. Utility is the aggregate defined in Section~\\ref{sec:protocol}. Full metrics are in Table~\\ref{tab:search-full}.}\n\\label{tab:search}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\nConfiguration & Utility & Per-seed utility (11 / 12) & MAE rand / strong & Spearman strong \\\\\n\\midrule\nLinear model-assisted & 0.9381 & 0.9369 / 0.9393 & 0.0180 / 0.0389 & 0.771 \\\\\nStack (selected) & 0.9452 & 0.9423 / 0.9480 & 0.0185 / 0.0359 & 0.816 \\\\\nStack + tail calibration & 0.9445 & 0.9423 / 0.9468 & 0.0185 / 0.0362 & 0.812 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Search versus held-out performance}\nThe selected configuration's utility is 0.9452 on the development benchmarks and 0.9314 on the held-out benchmarks. The benchmark sets, seeds, and number of seeds differ, so we report the difference without a causal explanation. The MAE gain over the random mean is also larger on development benchmarks ($+0.0086$) than on held-out benchmarks ($+0.0045$). % @evidence[[derived/metrics_summary.json#search.c02.delta_mae_vs_random_mean]]\nDevelopment scores are proxies that influenced which configuration was frozen; held-out scores are the appropriate basis for any claim.\n\n\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n\\label{sec:discussion}\n\\paragraph{What the evidence supports.} The stacked configuration has lower point-estimate MAE than three matched baselines on five held-out benchmarks, predictions that degrade as expected under both negative controls (each a single episode; no pass/fail criterion for the controls is evidenced), and shows a large degradation for stronger-than-source targets. It does not establish a statistically resolved improvement over a random-subset mean, a superiority claim, or generalization to other benchmarks, budgets or score types. The evaluator's ``qualified'' label is not a superiority or generalization claim, and no gate-level diagnostics are available to interpret it further.\n\n\\paragraph{Attribution.} The configuration differs from the random-mean baseline in four ways at once: items are selected using source data, a source-fitted calibration maps subset means to full accuracy, latent-model predictions are blended in, and the regime label selects the validation folds. No experiment isolates these. The gain over random mean could come from calibration alone, from item selection, or from the latent models; the benefit of the Fisher design under upper-tail reference abilities, in particular, is untested. The regime label is not given to the baselines, so the candidate has information they lack; this should be kept in mind when reading the comparison.\n\n\\paragraph{Threats to validity.} (i) Five benchmarks and three seeds make the bootstrap coarse; a larger panel might show a clear gain or none. (ii) Targets within an episode are few for some benchmarks, so Spearman and Kendall are noisy per episode. (iii) The strong-target regime is one definition (above the 0.7 accuracy quantile, sources below it) and need not represent real future models. (iv) Baselines are the evaluator's re-implementations with fixed settings; stronger tuned versions might narrow the gap. (v) Selection of the configuration used development scores from two seeds with within-variant spread comparable to between-variant differences. (vi) Candidate diagnostics are marked unverified.\n\n\\paragraph{Practical reading.} For a user who only needs coarse model scores on benchmarks resembling the historical pool, the random-target numbers (MAE 0.0179, Spearman 0.956 at $k=100$) indicate that 100 items can recover full accuracy to within about two percentage points on average and keep rankings largely intact. % @evidence[[derived/metrics_summary.json#final.mae_random_targets]]\nFor a user who must rank systems that exceed everything in the historical pool, the strong-target numbers (MAE 0.0409, Spearman 0.730) indicate that subset predictions alone should be treated as screening information, with direct evaluation reserved for the decisions that matter. Whether the stacked configuration or a plain random-subset mean is preferable for this purpose cannot be decided from our evidence, because their difference is smaller than the uncertainty we measured; the random mean is simpler, needs no source data, and has no regime-dependent tuning.\n\n\\paragraph{Relation to published numbers.} We do not compare against numbers reported in earlier papers: protocols differ in benchmark sets, target definitions, item budgets and metrics, and our baselines are re-implementations with fixed settings rather than the original code. Conclusions here are limited to the matched comparison on the evaluator's episodes.\n\n\\paragraph{What would resolve the comparison.} The width of the MAE-gain interval (0.0186) is about four times its point estimate (0.0045), so the held-out panel is too small to distinguish a gain of this size from zero. A decisive test would need more benchmarks and seeds than the five and three used here, paired in the same way, together with per-regime baseline results so that any gain can be located in the in-range or out-of-range regime. We expect, but have not verified, that the sign of the gain is more stable than its magnitude.\n\n\\paragraph{Not tested.} A sweep over the item budget $k$, additional seeds, single-factor ablations (latent rank, prior shrinkage, logistic versus linear model, Fisher versus stratified design, stacking versus a single estimator), temporal or model-family splits, and non-binary scores were not run. These are the experiments needed to attribute any gain to a mechanism.\n\n\\section{Conclusion}\nWe evaluated a source-validated stacked latent-ability estimator with Fisher D-optimal item design for predicting a new model's full-benchmark accuracy from 100 items. On five held-out benchmarks it had the lowest point-estimate MAE among the compared estimators (0.0294 versus 0.0339 for the random-subset mean) and its predictions degraded as expected under both negative controls (single episodes; no pass/fail criterion is evidenced), but the interval for its gain over the best baseline spans zero, so the improvement is not statistically established. The largest measured difference is that targets stronger than every source model are predicted about twice as badly (MAE 0.0409, Spearman 0.730) as random targets (MAE 0.0179, Spearman 0.956), on both development and held-out benchmark groups (point estimates, without intervals, and possibly partly due to the narrower score range of strong targets), and the design aimed at upper-tail abilities did not close this gap. Practically, a user who needs to rank models stronger than the historical pool should not rely on source-matrix-based subset prediction without direct validation on a few full evaluations. Future work should test a budget sweep, single-factor ablations and per-regime baseline comparisons to learn which component, if any, helps, and whether item selection can be made robust for out-of-range targets.\n\n% IMPORTANT: \\clearpage flushes all pending floats before references so that\n% body-page counting excludes reference-only pages.  Do NOT remove.\n\\clearpage\n\\bibliography{references}\n\\bibliographystyle{paper}\n\n\\clearpage\n\\appendix\n\\section{Supplementary Information}\n\n\\subsection{Evaluation protocol and metric definitions}\nEach episode is identified by a seed, a benchmark and a regime. For random targets the evaluator draws as targets 20\\% of the $M$ models with results on the benchmark (rounded to the nearest integer, at least 5) and uses the rest as sources. For strong targets, the pool is the set of models with full accuracy at or above the 0.7 quantile; sources are models strictly below it, and the targets are drawn from the pool (at most the same number). Episodes require at least 40 sources and 5 targets. The source matrix is transferred in at most two chunks of bit-packed rows (\\texttt{source\\_evidence/evaluate\\_final.py}, \\texttt{MAX\\_LOAD\\_CHUNKS}, \\texttt{pack\\_bits}). The selector must return exactly $k{=}100$ distinct valid indices, and the predictor must return one finite prediction per target. Aggregation proceeds from seeds to benchmarks (equal weight) to regimes (0.5/0.5); failed or missing episodes would contribute worst-case values, but none occurred in either evaluation (22 and 32 episodes completed, 0 failed). The development benchmarks are IFEval, ARC-Challenge, MuSR, GSM8K and BBH; the held-out benchmarks are GPQA, MedQA, LegalBench, MMLU and MMLU-Pro. The number of observed candidate calls was 88 in the development evaluation and 128 in the final evaluation.\n\n\\subsection{Estimator details of the selected configuration}\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\textbf{Linear latent model:} centred source matrix, SVD of the $m\\times m$ Gram matrix, maximum rank 12; item residual variances are floored at $\\max(0.02,\\text{5th percentile})$. Rank grid $\\{1,2,3,5,8,12\\}$ and prior-scale grid $\\tau\\in\\{1,4,16\\}$.\n\\item \\textbf{Logistic latent model:} eight alternating Newton sweeps with L2 penalties 0.5 (loadings), 0.05 (intercepts) and 1.0 (abilities); steps clipped to $[-2,2]$; rank grid $\\{2,3,1,5\\}$ evaluated in that order under a 45-second budget for logistic fitting, after which remaining ranks are skipped; prior-scale grid $\\{1,4,16\\}$; target abilities by up to 15 Newton iterations.\n\\item \\textbf{Designs:} four linear designs (stratified; stratified$+$D-optimal half; D-optimal; $k$-means$+$D-optimal half) and three Fisher designs (pure; half stratified; half linear D-optimal), the latter built at logistic rank 3 with reference abilities from the top 10\\% of source abilities plus margins $\\{0,0.5,1.0\\}$ standard deviations.\n\\item \\textbf{Blend grid:} linear or logistic estimator blended with the calibrated mean with weights $\\{0,0.2,\\dots,1\\}$, plus NNLS stacks over the three best linear and three best logistic candidates per design.\n\\item \\textbf{Selection objective:} among the 15 lowest-MSE pseudo-target configurations, minimize $\\mathrm{RMSE}/\\mathrm{RMSE}_{\\min}+(1-\\overline{(\\rho+\\tau)/2})$, with the rank term averaged over folds.\n\\item \\textbf{Fallbacks:} if validation cannot run (fewer than ten source models) a conservative blend of 0.7 calibrated mean and 0.3 linear estimate on a hybrid design is used; any exception returns a stratified design with the calibrated mean.\n\\end{itemize}\nThe values above are constants and routines in \\texttt{source\\_evidence/candidate\\_program.py} (constant block and the functions \\texttt{fit\\_logistic\\_latent}, \\texttt{\\_make\\_folds}, \\texttt{\\_validate}, \\texttt{stack\\_weights}). The extension in Table~\\ref{tab:search} (\\texttt{source\\_evidence/extension\\_program.py}) additionally uses bagged logistic fits at ranks 2, 3 and 4, a Laplace posterior mean for item probabilities, and a ridge-shrunk monotone tail correction applied only above the source median accuracy, accepted only if it beats the incumbent on pseudo-targets.\n\n\\subsection{Development-benchmark results for all three configurations}\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Complete development-benchmark metrics (five benchmarks, seeds 11 and 12). Gains are positive when the configuration is better than the baseline. Rows: linear model-assisted estimator; selected stack; stack with tail calibration.}\n\\label{tab:search-full}\n\\footnotesize\n\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{3pt}\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\nMetric & Linear & Stack (selected) & Stack + tail \\\\\n\\midrule\nUtility & 0.9381 & 0.9452 & 0.9445 \\\\ % @evidence[[derived/metrics_summary.json#search.c01.combined_score]]\nMAE / RMSE & 0.0285 / 0.0347 & 0.0272 / 0.0336 & 0.0274 / 0.0339 \\\\\nSpearman / Kendall & 0.866 / 0.730 & 0.889 / 0.756 & 0.888 / 0.752 \\\\\nMAE random / strong & 0.0180 / 0.0389 & 0.0185 / 0.0359 & 0.0185 / 0.0362 \\\\\nSpearman random / strong & 0.962 / 0.771 & 0.963 / 0.816 & 0.963 / 0.812 \\\\\nMAE gain vs.\\ random mean & $+0.0073$ & $+0.0086$ & $+0.0084$ \\\\\nMAE gain vs.\\ anchor points & $+0.0096$ & $+0.0109$ & $+0.0107$ \\\\\nMAE gain vs.\\ IRT-style & $+0.0405$ & $+0.0418$ & $+0.0416$ \\\\\nUtility gain vs.\\ best baseline & $+0.0084$ & $+0.0155$ & $+0.0148$ \\\\\nPermuted: Spearman drop / MAE increase & 1.021 / 0.109 & 1.033 / 0.110 & 1.033 / 0.110 \\\\\nConstant: MAE increase & 0.077 & 0.077 & 0.077 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Final evaluation details}\nThe per-seed utilities of the selected configuration are 0.9257 (seed 9101), 0.9324 (seed 9102) and 0.9360 (seed 9103); their spread (0.0103) is comparable to the point-estimate utility gain over the best baseline (0.0093), consistent with the unresolved bootstrap interval. % @evidence[[derived/metrics_summary.json#final.per_seed_utility]]\nRecorded wall-clock time per seeded run was 150.8, 77.4 and 75.1 seconds respectively for the three seeds. The evaluator reports 128 candidate calls observed over 32 episodes. Utility differences against anchor points and IRT-style baselines are not accompanied by intervals in the retained outputs. The evaluation was declared \\emph{qualified} with completed status; candidate-side diagnostics are recorded as unverified.\n\n\\subsection{Reproducibility, data provenance and seeds}\n\\textbf{Data.} The per-item matrices are the public score files released with \\citet{Zhang2025HowBP}, pinned to a fixed repository commit (55faff49\\ldots{}), derived from the Open LLM Leaderboard and HELM. \\textbf{Seeds.} Development 11, 12; final 9101, 9102, 9103; bootstrap 20260101; the evaluated configuration seeds its own generators from the episode seed. \\textbf{Software.} The task requirements file (\\texttt{source\\_evidence/requirements.txt}) pins numpy 2.5.4, scipy 1.18.1, scikit-learn 1.9.1 and joblib 1.6.0; the evaluated configuration sets eight BLAS threads by default (\\texttt{candidate\\_program.py}, header). \\textbf{Figures.} Figures~\\ref{fig:main} and \\ref{fig:gap} are produced by a single plotting script from the evaluator outputs and the retained metrics files; the overview figure is a schematic and contains no data. \\textbf{Code.} The numbers derive from a single-file estimator and the evaluator script, whose baselines are the evaluator's own implementations; copies of the sources are supplied under \\texttt{source\\_evidence/} and are not linked in this anonymized draft. We did not access the raw leaderboard details, which are partly gated.\n\n\\subsection{LLM Usage}\nLarge language models were used throughout this work. An LLM-based research agent proposed the estimator variants, implemented them, ran the development and final evaluations through the provided harness, selected related work from a starter list and retrieved references, and drafted this manuscript. All reported numbers originate from the evaluator outputs and retained metric files, not from model generation; the LLM did not generate data. The agent did not access any content excluded by the task rules.\n\n\\end{document}\n","numLines":148,"startLine":128,"totalLines":275}}}
